  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter another dry day with departing high pressure today, a \nbroad upper level trough will set up over the Great Lakes by \nTuesday morning. A deep 990s mb surface low over Canada will \ndrag the next frontal system through. A surge of moisture ahead \nof the leading warm front will help support scattered showers \nand isolated thunderstorms. Following the warm front, a cold \nfront will eventually push through, though the timing of its \npassage remains a bit uncertain and may lag. A pre-frontal\ntrough ahead of the primary front may turn out to the be the\nmain focus for thunderstorms Tuesday afternoon-early evening. \nModel guidance varies greatly on instability Tuesday afternoon, \nranging from just a few hundred to over 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE. \nStrong flow aloft will enhance the shear with values expected to\nbe 40 to 50 kts based on most guidance. Even with the wide \nrange of instability values, conditions look favorable for \nanother round of strong to severe storms. While all hazards look\nto be possible, the highest confidence is with strong gusts \nbeing the main concern. The Storm Prediction Center maintains a\nsevere thunderstorm risk for our region.\n\nPrecipitable water values are projected into the 1.5 to 2 \ninches range and warm cloud depths will be 10 to 12 thousand \nfeet, which would favor heavy rainfall at times. Training of \nthunderstorms would also be a concern. If the pre-frontal \ntrough causes most convection to exit, than the progressive \nnature of the cells may mitigate the excessive rainfall threat.\nHowever, if additional showers-embedded storms linger back with\nthe front itself then there could be repeated batches to cause \npotential issues. Weather Prediction Center continues to \nhighlight the area in at least a lower end risk for excessive \nrainfall Tuesday. Given that some locations received over 2 \ninches of rainfall with the last storm system, antecedent \nconditions will be more favorable for localized hydro issues in \npoor drainage areas despite a couple of dry days in between. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe upper level trough will dig into the Eastern Great Lakes \nand with a trailing shortwave Wednesday that could support \nadditional showers in addition to cooler temperatures. High \npressure and drier air will build in as the trough slides east, \ncutting off any remaining showers by Wednesday night.\n\nSurface high pressure will be in control for the second half of the \nweek with generally dry conditions. Temperatures will still be\nsomewhat below average Thursday but will trend modestly warmer\nby the weekend with highs mostly in the 80s as weak ridging\noccurs aloft. There is uncertainty for whether weak waves\nembedded in the ridge could manage small shower/thunder chances\nby late in the weekend; the dryness of the air may be too much\nto overcome. While it remains uncertain if wildfire smoke will \nreturn late this week, the pattern of a ridge out west and \ntrough in the east would be favorable flow for off-and-on smoke\nat times over the Northeast.",
